#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# champernowne_number

#% text
# `champernowne_number(b)` is the number whose base-`b` digits after the point are the integers 1, 2, 3, ... written one after another; `champernowne_number()` is the base-10 number 0.123456789101112...

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `n(champernowne_number(b), p)` gives `p` digits; the number is irrational and transcendental.

#% item
# The call stays symbolic until `n` is applied.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
champernowne_number()
#% output
#= champernowne_number(10)

#% cell
n(champernowne_number(), 25)
#% output
#= 0.123456789101112131415161718192

#% cell
n(champernowne_number(3), 25)
#% output
#= 0.59895816753843399250017221793

#% cell
n(champernowne_number(2))
#% output
#= 0.8622401258680545

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `integer_digits`, `from_digits`, `real_digits`
